to you on Zoom, Wyatt.

00:01:05.098 --> 00:01:06.366
It's great to have you
in the studio.

00:01:06.366 --> 00:01:08.001
And, Kurt, it's been a while
since you were here.

00:01:08.001 --> 00:01:09.268
-I know.
-But you're a man of your word.

00:01:09.268 --> 00:01:10.569
Last time you were here,

00:01:10.569 --> 00:01:12.971
we were talking about
your vineyard and your wine,

00:01:12.971 --> 00:01:15.374
and you promised to bring me
some wine, and you did.

00:01:15.374 --> 00:01:16.576
-Well, you guys
figured that out.

00:01:16.576 --> 00:01:18.611
-So we're gonna have some.
-He's ready to go.

00:01:18.611 --> 00:01:21.981
[ Cheers and applause ]
-I have some, uh...

00:01:21.981 --> 00:01:23.216
Well, first, cheers
to you guys here.

00:01:23.216 --> 00:01:24.316
Happy new year.
-Yeah.

00:01:24.316 --> 00:01:25.518
Hey, it's great
to see you again.

00:01:25.518 --> 00:01:27.921
-Thank you, thank you.
-This is Gogi Wine,

00:01:27.921 --> 00:01:30.222
but is this La Mer?
-This is La Mer. Yeah.

00:01:30.222 --> 00:01:33.159
-La Mer is named
after Meredith Hagner,

00:01:33.159 --> 00:01:35.628
Wyatt Russell's wife.
-Yes. Your lovely wife --

00:01:35.628 --> 00:01:37.664
an incredibly talented
and funny actress.

00:01:37.664 --> 00:01:39.698
-Smarter, funnier.
Every adjective you could use.

00:01:39.698 --> 00:01:41.667
-Mother of our
seventh grandchild.

00:01:41.667 --> 00:01:44.403
-You have seven grandchild --
-And soon to be eight.

00:01:44.403 --> 00:01:45.704
-You're about to have eight.
Congratulations.

00:01:45.704 --> 00:01:46.772
I know you're about
to have your second.

00:01:46.772 --> 00:01:48.074
-Yeah.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:48.074 --> 00:01:49.475
Life's about to
change big time.

00:01:49.475 --> 00:01:52.812
-Real quick, obviously
you've seen your father

00:01:52.812 --> 00:01:55.948
as a grandparent with other --
your siblings' grandkids --

00:01:55.948 --> 00:01:57.116
or their children.
-Yes.

00:01:57.116 --> 00:01:58.684
-How is he as a grandparent?

00:01:58.684 --> 00:02:02.922
-He's -- He's awesome,
'cause he can do all of the --

00:02:02.922 --> 00:02:05.558
all of the hard sort of lessons
that, like,

00:02:05.558 --> 00:02:07.159
sometimes
I don't want to have to do.

00:02:07.159 --> 00:02:12.298
But luckily he doesn't hide any
of who he is to who my son is.

00:02:12.298 --> 00:02:15.434
So there was, like --
We were going on a street

00:02:15.434 --> 00:02:17.670
when I was living in Vancouver
shooting "Monarch,"

00:02:17.670 --> 00:02:20.539
and Buddy wants to test going
on the street, and my dad goes,

00:02:20.539 --> 00:02:22.408
"No, no, no!"
-No, no!

00:02:22.408 --> 00:02:24.177
-Buddy lost it.
He was crying.

00:02:24.177 --> 00:02:25.945
And I'm like, "Well, I don't
know. It's a good lesson."

00:02:25.945 --> 00:02:27.880
Comes back, he's like,
"What does Gogi say?"

00:02:27.880 --> 00:02:31.016
Buddy goes, "No, no, no,"
points it's the street.

00:02:31.016 --> 00:02:33.185
And I was like, "Yes!
You got it." Like thanks, Pop.

00:02:33.185 --> 00:02:36.455
-So, wait. So, is Gogi
both the name of the wine

00:02:36.455 --> 00:02:38.157
and your grandparent name?

00:02:38.157 --> 00:02:40.393
-Yes.
See, I grew up as Gogo.

00:02:40.393 --> 00:02:41.861
-Okay.
-My middle name was Vogel.

00:02:41.861 --> 00:02:43.195
I couldn't pronounce it
when I was a little kid,

00:02:43.195 --> 00:02:44.463
so I was Gogo.

00:02:44.463 --> 00:02:45.932
-So everybody called you Gogo.
-I'm Gogo.

00:02:45.932 --> 00:02:50.336
And then one day, when I was
very early on with Goldie,

00:02:50.336 --> 00:02:53.305
my dad, who called me Goge,
said, "Hey, Goge,"

00:02:53.305 --> 00:02:56.108
and we both turned around
and she looked, and she said,

00:02:56.108 --> 00:02:57.209
"How did you know my nickname?"

00:02:57.209 --> 00:02:59.178
She grew up as Gogo.
-No way!

00:02:59.178 --> 00:03:03.115
-My nickname Gogo turned
into, via my sisters,

00:03:03.115 --> 00:03:06.252
a lot of different versions
of Gogo, Goginshki...

00:03:06.252 --> 00:03:07.920
-Yeah.
-...and Gogi.

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:10.123
Gogi was the sort of
what I became

00:03:10.123 --> 00:03:13.225
to all the grandkids
and to all my family.

00:03:13.225 --> 00:03:15.327
-It's really funny that
at a very young age

00:03:15.327 --> 00:03:17.130
you had a perfect
grandfather name.

00:03:17.130 --> 00:03:18.864
-[ Laughs ]

00:03:18.864 --> 00:03:21.601
-Like you've grown into Gogo
very well.

00:03:21.601 --> 00:03:22.968
Because, like, when you meet
like a four year old

00:03:22.968 --> 00:03:25.371
called Gogo, you're like,
"What's this kid getting up to?"

00:03:25.371 --> 00:03:26.539
[ Laughter ]

00:03:26.539 --> 00:03:29.142
-So it's Gogo and Gogi
to all the grandkids

00:03:29.142 --> 00:03:31.344
and...
-And now what is it like

00:03:31.344 --> 00:03:33.012
watching Wyatt as a father?

00:03:33.012 --> 00:03:34.213
How's that transformation?

00:03:34.213 --> 00:03:35.514
-That's a problem. But...
-Yeah.

00:03:35.514 --> 00:03:37.317
-[ Laughs ]
-I mean, obviously his kids

00:03:37.317 --> 00:03:38.617
don't know streets are bad.

00:03:38.617 --> 00:03:39.652
[ Indistinct ]

00:03:39.652 --> 00:03:41.988
-Guy's an idiot.
-[ Indistinct ]

00:03:41.988 --> 00:03:43.823
I got to say,
they're great parents.

00:03:43.823 --> 00:03:45.825
No, I thought I was going to say
he's a great grandparent

00:03:45.825 --> 00:03:47.226
because he's hands-off.
-[ Laughs ]

00:03:47.226 --> 00:03:49.696
But it's like...
Uh, no, it's fun to watch,

00:03:49.696 --> 00:03:51.030
it really is,
with all of our kids.

00:03:51.030 --> 00:03:53.732
with Ollie and Kate's kids,

00:03:53.732 --> 00:03:56.669
and Uncle Boston comes over
to everybody's house.

00:03:56.669 --> 00:03:58.904
It's -- You know,
we are very fortunate.

00:03:58.904 --> 00:04:00.739
-Uh, obviously,
Wyatt looks up to you

00:04:00.739 --> 00:04:02.408
because he also has
his own cocktail.

00:04:02.408 --> 00:04:04.243
-Oh, yeah. That's right.
-It's not wine.

00:04:04.243 --> 00:04:05.845
This is Lake Hour.
-Lake Hour.

00:04:05.845 --> 00:04:08.081
-This is a vodka cocktail
in a can.

00:04:08.081 --> 00:04:09.215
-That's right.
-Yes.

00:04:09.215 --> 00:04:11.751
Honeysuckle...
uh, honeysuckle ginger.

00:04:11.751 --> 00:04:13.686
-It is born here in New York.

00:04:13.686 --> 00:04:16.288
-Alright.
-Ooh!

00:04:16.288 --> 00:04:18.224
Ooh, we're moving
right along here.

00:04:18.224 --> 00:04:19.993
-I assumed you start
with the wine,

00:04:19.993 --> 00:04:21.193
and then you move
to the drink in a cam.

00:04:21.193 --> 00:04:22.628
-[ Laughs ]
-Yeah. I was gonna make a joke.

00:04:22.628 --> 00:04:24.897
I was like, "The guy in that
clip needed a Lake Hour."

00:04:24.897 --> 00:04:26.432
-[ Indistinct ]

00:04:26.432 --> 00:04:27.500
-Yeah, there you go.
-Cheers.

00:04:27.500 --> 00:04:29.301
-Two-fister here.
Great seeing you guys.

00:04:29.301 --> 00:04:30.937
-Cheers.
-Cheers. Good to see you guys.

00:04:30.937 --> 00:04:33.439
-Hey, have a happy new!

00:04:33.439 --> 00:04:35.007
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:35.007 --> 00:04:36.141
-Delicious.
-Yeah.

00:04:36.141 --> 00:04:37.910
Nice and light
and refreshing.

00:04:37.910 --> 00:04:40.713
-I was very jealous
when we spoke

00:04:40.713 --> 00:04:42.815
because your three-year-old's
name is Buddy Prine,

00:04:42.815 --> 00:04:44.817
which is a fantastic name.
-Thank you.

00:04:44.817 --> 00:04:48.521
-And, uh, his birthday,
December 26th.

00:04:48.521 --> 00:04:50.423
I sympathize with him.
December 28th.

00:04:50.423 --> 00:04:51.724
-Oh, really?
-So I know the feeling.

00:04:51.724 --> 00:04:53.192
Although, the 26th
seems even worse.

00:04:53.192 --> 00:04:55.561
Does he appreciate...?
-No, no, dude.

00:04:55.561 --> 00:04:57.763
So -- So it was -- It was --

00:04:57.763 --> 00:05:01.434
We do Christmas, 25th of Chris--
25th of, you know.

00:05:01.434 --> 00:05:02.902
We wake up, we do the Christmas.

00:05:02.902 --> 00:05:06.339
It's nuts. It's a disaster
most of the day.

00:05:06.339 --> 00:05:07.340
It's great most of the day.

00:05:07.340 --> 00:05:08.907
Goes to sleep, wake up.

00:05:08.907 --> 00:05:11.177
Mer and I are like,
"Okay, Buddy.

00:05:11.177 --> 00:05:13.846
Buddy, it's your birthday!"

00:05:13.846 --> 00:05:15.114
Like, "Gotta get excited."

00:05:15.114 --> 00:05:16.616
And he goes -- And he's three,

00:05:16.616 --> 00:05:18.451
and he goes, "Ugh! Not now."

00:05:18.451 --> 00:05:20.686
[ Laughter ]

00:05:20.686 --> 00:05:22.088
We're like, "You got it!

00:05:22.088 --> 00:05:25.024
That's the right answer!
You're learning well.

00:05:25.024 --> 00:05:26.692
Like, no one wants to celebrate
your birthday."

00:05:26.692 --> 00:05:29.162
-A three-year-old who actually
has an understanding of excess

00:05:29.162 --> 00:05:30.863
is pretty great.
-Yes, exactly.

00:05:30.863 --> 00:05:33.332
He's like, "Enough is enough."
-Enough is enough.

00:05:33.332 --> 00:05:35.768
-Well, I think it was --
a very cool thing for him

00:05:35.768 --> 00:05:37.836
is that his dad
and his grandfather

00:05:37.836 --> 00:05:38.838
are in
a television show together.

00:05:38.838 --> 00:05:40.172
We're going to talk about
that next.

00:05:40.172 --> 00:05:43.076
We'll be right back
with Kurt and Wyatt after this.

